    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Selling is the art of persuading individuals to purchase products or services.</p><p>Known as <strong> personal selling</strong>, this process involves direct, two-way communication between a salesperson and a customer, focusing on fulfilling the customer’s needs and securing a sale.</p><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>The Selling Process</strong></h2><p>The selling process consists of several key stages, each with specific objectives and strategies:</p><table border="2" style="width:450px"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Prospecting</strong></td><td>Identifying potential customers who may need or benefit from the product or service.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pre-approach</strong></td><td>Gathering information about the prospect to tailor the sales approach effectively.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Approach</strong></td><td>Making initial contact with the prospect, setting a confident and prepared tone for the interaction.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Presentation</strong></td><td>Explaining the features, advantages, and benefits of the product or service in alignment with the prospect's needs.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Handling Objections</strong></td><td>Addressing any concerns or hesitations the prospect may have, using politeness and understanding to provide clarity and reassurance.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Closing the Sale</strong></td><td>Concluding the sales process by securing the customer's commitment to purchase.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Follow-up</strong></td><td>Engaging post-sale to ensure customer satisfaction, addressing any issues, and laying the groundwork for future sales.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Effective selling requires a variety of skills that enhance the salesperson's ability to connect with customers and meet their needs.</p><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Skills Include</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Researching Prospects</strong>: Understanding the prospect's background, needs, and how they align with your offerings.</li><li><strong>Planning Meetings</strong>: Structuring interactions to lead to a commitment or advancement in the sales process.</li><li><strong>Creating Rapport</strong>: Building a connection and trust with the prospect, demonstrating genuine interest and care.</li><li><strong>Asking Questions</strong>: Inquiring effectively to uncover the prospect's true needs and how best to meet them.</li><li><strong>Active Listening</strong>: Paying close attention to the customer's responses to understand their perspective and requirements.</li><li><strong>Presenting Solutions</strong>: Tailoring your product or service presentation to address the identified needs of the prospect.</li><li><strong>Asking for Commitment</strong>: Encouraging the prospect to make a purchase decision, guiding them towards agreeing to the sale.</li><li><strong>Building Relationships</strong>: Developing long-term connections that extend beyond one-time transactions, aiming for ongoing business and referrals.</li></ul>`
